# site.zd — a multi-page site (spec §14G.2).
#
# Five URLs out of one program, and the compiler writes one document per
# URL:
#
#   /            /writing        /writing/routing
#   /writing/folding             /404
#
# Three things it exists to demonstrate.
#
#   1. **A route is a `choice` plus a bijection onto URLs.** `Site` is
#      declared exactly as a `choice` is, `when page` dispatches on it
#      exactly as a `when` dispatches on any choice, and a route
#      parameter is a variant field. Nothing downstream needed a second
#      notion of a variant.
#
#   2. **`address` is immutable and `Link` is the only navigation.**
#      Clicking a `Link` is a browser navigation: this program instance
#      ends and a new one begins at the target document. `client` state
#      therefore does not survive a navigation — which is not a rule, it
#      is what "a new program instance" means. Try `set page to Home` and
#      the compiler names the rule.
#
#   3. **Per-page splitting falls out of the fold.** Because the address
#      signal is immutable, its value is a compile-time constant for each
#      emitted document, so `when page` has exactly one live arm per
#      page. `/writing/routing` does not contain the home page's markup,
#      the index's list, or the other post's text.
#
# What it wanted and could not have: `Nav` is written as a `component`
# below rather than as an element, because §14G.2's own milestone-7
# example uses element names that do not exist. `Link` *is* added here,
# because routing without navigation is not routing — see the report.

use "./content" for slugs, tagline, titleOf, bodyOf

# `slugs` is `static`, which is what makes the route parameter below
# enumerable — and enumerable is all it makes it. `slug` is **Untrusted**,
# with an `in` clause or without one (§21.7.6, §21.6 item 18b).
#
# §18.1 semantics 5 once read the other way: the build renders one document
# per value, so reaching `/writing/routing` proves `slug` is a value the
# build chose. That is true and it is the wrong proposition. The match
# proves membership in the operator's enumeration, not provenance — the
# visitor still chooses which URL to visit, and choosing among published
# values is a choice. §19.11.3 spends it: 160 prerenderable URLs, both `in`
# collections public and static exactly as routing requires, reconstruct a
# card number one digit at a time with every parameter Trusted and nothing
# endorsed. Two static lists of digits and positions are the shape of an
# ordinary pagination index; neither is suspicious alone.
#
# So semantics 5 is deleted rather than narrowed, and indexing a `trusted`
# collection by `slug` is refused with E-INT-02. That reads as a regression
# to whoever hits it first, because semantics 5 promised the opposite — but
# the rejection is a true one, and its name is IDOR.
route Site
    Home       is "/"
    Writing    is "/writing"
    Post       is "/writing" with slug is Text in slugs

# Immutable. Written by the browser at load; never by the program.
state page is client Option of Site starting address

component Nav
    Row padding is 8
        Link Home, padding is 8
            Text "home"
        Link Writing, padding is 8
            Text "writing"

component PostLink with slug
    Row padding is 4
        Link (Post with slug is slug)
            Text (titleOf with slug is slug)

view
    Column
        Nav

        when page
            None
                Heading "Nothing here"
                Text "That URL is not part of this site."
                Link Home
                    Text "go home"
            Some with here
                when here
                    Home
                        Heading "A site in one file"
                        Text (tagline with count is 5)
                    Writing
                        Heading "Writing"
                        each slug in slugs
                            PostLink slug
                    Post with slug
                        Heading (titleOf with slug is slug)
                        Text (bodyOf with slug is slug)
                        Link Writing
                            Text "back"
